A||||17||Bruiser||I can't stand it anymore, too much vibrations!||||6||
Z||000000||Bruiser||12-20-2001||03:18 AM||||So I got this 85 SE, with 235s all round. Right as you hit 65 km/h'ish, it starts vibrating like hell, and then dies off at around 75 km/h. More specifically the back right, but I think its noticable from the back left as well. The fronts seem to be fine. When you take a corner, it shuts up, and then starts again when you straiten out. It makes a hell of a lot more noise than it does vibration. But what I was wondering, has anyone else had this problem? And what can I do about it besides getting skinnier tires?<P>I haven't had the chance to get it up on the lift in at school and take the tires off, but I'm assuming its the cause. I'm hoping its the tires anyway. I think they may be rubbing on something like the springs. There wasn't any noticable rubbing on the inner fenders (and if there is I'm cutting it out). But I sure hope its not something to do with the transaxle... might be time to upgrade from a 4, to a 5 speed if thats the case.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Thanks for any help.||||reg||6
Z||000001||tesmith66||12-20-2001||07:36 AM||||Welcome to the forum!<P>First, rotate the tires and see if the vibration changes any (severity, location, etc.). If it still does it, but from a different corner, then have the wheels balanced. If that doesn't help, check for a bad tire or bent rim. If that doesn't do it, you may have a bad wheel bearing. <P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||12-20-2001||07:41 AM||||Could be tires are out of balance.<BR>Also, if the rear toe is out of spec, it can cause the tires to wear unevenly (scallop or cup). Makes a hell of a roar, going down the road. Sounds like monster mudders. <BR>The tires also might be hitting the rear spring perch, but it seems like that would happen at all speeds.<BR>You might have a bad rear wheel bearing. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bruiser||12-20-2001||11:07 AM||||Alright, thanks guys. I'll try and get the thing up on the lift sometime to day and get the tires off. I'll let ya'll know what happens.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Mach10||12-20-2001||01:04 PM||||Vibration that change at different speeds? TIRE BALANCE. That'll be it. Trust me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||riceboyfiero||12-20-2001||03:02 PM||||sounds like you might have balance problems and a wheel bearing some where...weave the car and if the noise goes away or intesifies at any points its prob a bearing..if it goes away when your pulling left then its the left if it intesifies when you pull left then its your right and vis virsa...now all you guys give me help on my crank work!..please||||reg||1
Z||000006||Freshj||12-21-2001||01:34 AM||||I'm having the exact same problem, turns out my pass. rear lower contol arm frt. bushing is gone letting the whole wheel toe in and out a few inches as it pleases. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bruiser||12-21-2001||03:34 AM||||****  I didn't check the bushings. So I got it up on the lift, balanced the tires, its still there. What I realised though is that its almost 99% percent noise. Kind of like a humming roar sort of deal. Not a much vibration at all. But anyway, I'll check the bushings tomorrow. If it ain't that I'll assume its the bearing (what else is there anyway). <P>Oh yeah, the tires do rub on the spring, but I suspect its only under a hella-load, cause the springs are far enough up on the strut that they don't touch when theres nothing in the car.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Old Lar||12-21-2001||05:57 AM||||It could also be the CV joint .  CV join boot in good shap? no tears, full of grease?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Butter||12-21-2001||03:41 PM||||Bruiser,<BR>If its vibration it will more than likely be a balance problem or a tire out of round.<P>If its more of a noise problem that seems to go through the car more than likely its a wheel bearing.  When the wheel bearing started down on my Fiero it was first noticeable at higher speeds then got worse at lower speeds.  I found it to be the right rear wheel bearing by listening to the noise get louder when cornering to the left.<P>Just noticed you said the tire has been rubbing the spring.  Tires do grow somewhat in size as the speed increases.<P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Bruiser||12-22-2001||05:06 AM||||Hmm. Well I think I'm gunna go ahead and replace the back wheel bearings, and regrease the fronts anyway. Also, when we had it up on the lift, my friend said one of the boots on my right CV was cracked (the inside boot looks brand new, so he must have meant the outside boot). I took a quick look before I put the wheel back on, but I didn't see anything. But I'll start with the bearings (and maybe tighten up the slop in my steering while I'm at it), and if the noise pursists, I'll do the CV boot as well. But I'm almost certain its the bearing.<P>Hehe BTW, my car is identical to the one in yer sig Butter, cept I got the sun roof.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Bruiser (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||West Coast Fiero||12-22-2001||06:05 AM||||Bruiser, would you by any chance happen to have a Getrag 5spd in your car?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bruiser||12-22-2001||02:55 PM||||Naw its a Muncie 4.||||reg||6
Z||000013||theogre||12-22-2001||03:16 PM||||check over that half shaft more....<P>A bent shaft or a bad CV can also cause vibrations like you've discribed above. If the outer CVs loose their marbles then they can be quite out of balance before they totally fail.<P>A bad bearing in the tranny could also do it. A bad bearing allows to much play. That can let the inner CV run off center.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ed||12-22-2001||06:48 PM||||A "free" check is to merely put some other tires on.  One of our club members had trouble with his car "hunting" or following every little rut in the road.  We put my tires on and it went away.<P>His tires were "too big!"  <P>Try the simple and free things first in fixing cars if you can.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Bruiser||12-23-2001||03:40 AM||||Well I did rotate them, so its not the indevidual tire. I have 14"s and 235s all round, cept on the front right, where its a 14" and 215 (I dunno why, it came like that). I was planning to put this BFGoodrich tire that I have on it, but I could try putting it on the back right first. But I don't think that would explain why ONLY the back right makes the noise and the left doesn't.<P>But come to think of it, I think my car may exhibit a bit of that hunting thing you mentioned. When I had the car up on the lift, the front wheels had some play in them though ,probably more than the recomended 2000ths of an inch, but not an extreme amount. Just enough to make the click. <P>At any rate I guess I have to do a thorough examination of the running gear.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000016||Jncomutt||12-23-2001||06:08 PM||||Hey guys, what exactly is involved in changing a front wheel wheel bearing?  I've never done any such work but I think it needs it.  I steering wheel shakes and when theres any slight turning to the left, I hear a clanking or knocking type noise and feel it on the 'dead pedal'  I Just had the car aligned and tires balanced, that didn't help at all.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||12-23-2001||06:45 PM||||take each wheel and have it balanced. while its turning slow watch the sides of tire and rim for wobble (bent rim or bad tire). if thats ok watch across the tread to see if has any high or low spots as it turns (runout). If it all checks out ok and balance is ok, then start looking other places like wheel bearings, bushings. I bought 3 sets of brand new tires on my Mercedes before I finally got a set of 'round' ones. Quality control on tires these days sucks bad. ||||reg||1
